<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-row-reduction" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Row Reduction Algorithm</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      We now have all the pieces: elementary row operations preserve the solution set, and
      echelon forms make the solution set easy to read off. The row reduction algorithm
      ties them together into a procedure that solves <em>any</em> system of linear
      equations.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-the-algorithm">
    <title>The Algorithm</title>

    <algorithm xml:id="alg-row-reduction">
      <title>The Row Reduction Algorithm</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Starting with the whole matrix, choose the leftmost column containing
            a nonzero entry among the remaining rows. If all remaining rows are zero,
            the forward phase is finished.</p></li>
            <li><p>Interchange rows if necessary to put a nonzero entry of that column
            in the top remaining row. This entry is the next pivot.</p></li>
            <li><p>Use row replacements to create zeros below the pivot.</p></li>
            <li><p>Continue with the rows strictly below the pivot row and the columns
            strictly to the right of the pivot column. Repeat steps 1<ndash/>3 until
            no rows or columns remain, or the remaining rows are zero. The matrix
            is now in row-echelon form.</p></li>
            <li><p>Beginning with the rightmost pivot and working left, divide each
            pivot row by its pivot to make it <m>1</m>, then use row replacements to
            create zeros above it. The result is reduced row-echelon form.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>

    <remark xml:id="rem-forward-backward">
      <p>
        Note that steps 1<ndash />4 will produce a row-echelon matrix, and step 5 will
        convert it to a reduced row-echelon matrix:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Steps 1<ndash />4:</term> the <term>forward phase</term>, to obtain
              a row-echelon form (REF).
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Step 5:</term> the <term>backward phase</term>, to obtain the reduced
              row-echelon form (RREF).
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        The forward phase involves choices <mdash /> which nonzero entry to swap into the
        pivot row, for instance <mdash /> and different choices produce different
        row-echelon matrices. The backward phase removes all such differences: by
        <xref ref="thm-rref-unique" text="type-global"/>, everyone who row reduces the same
        matrix arrives at the same reduced row-echelon form.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-three-outcomes">
    <title>The Three Possible Outcomes</title>

    <p>
      The three graphical possibilities of
      <xref ref="fig-three-possibilities" text="type-global" /> correspond to three
      outcomes of the algorithm. The next three examples illustrate each in turn. Each
      system has three equations in three unknowns, so each equation is a plane in
      space and the solution set is whatever the three planes have in common; the
      interactive figures let you rotate the planes and see it.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="eg-unique-solution">
      <title>A System with a Unique Solution</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve the system
          <md>
            <mrow>
              x_1 + x_2 + 2x_3 \amp = -1
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              x_2 + 2x_1 + 3x_3 \amp = 0
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              x_3 - 2x_2 \amp = 2
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          First rewrite each equation with the variables in the order
          <m>x_1, x_2, x_3</m> and form the augmented matrix. The forward phase gives
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\
                2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp -2 \amp 1 \amp 2
              \end{array} \right]
              \amp \xrightarrow{-2r_1 + r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp -2 \amp 1 \amp 2
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-2r_2 + r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 3 \amp -2
              \end{array} \right],
            </mrow>
          </md>
          which is a row-echelon matrix.
        </p>
        <p>
          Now the backward phase:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{\frac{1}{3} r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -\frac{2}{3}
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow[-2r_3 + r_1]{r_3 + r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp \frac{1}{3} \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \amp \frac{4}{3} \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -\frac{2}{3}
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp \frac{1}{3} \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -\frac{4}{3} \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -\frac{2}{3}
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-r_2 + r_1}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp \frac{5}{3} \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -\frac{4}{3} \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -\frac{2}{3}
              \end{array} \right],
            </mrow>
          </md>
          a reduced row-echelon matrix.
        </p>
        <p>
          Reading off the rows,
          <md>
            x_1 = \frac{5}{3}, \qquad x_2 = -\frac{4}{3}, \qquad x_3 = -\frac{2}{3},
          </md>
          a unique solution. Every column of the coefficient matrix is a pivot column, so
          there are no free variables.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-unique-planes">
          <caption>
            The three planes meet at exactly one point, <m>(5/3,-4/3,-2/3)</m>, the
            unique solution. Drag the picture (or use the camera sliders) to rotate it,
            and switch planes off to see that any <em>two</em> of them meet in a whole
            dashed line: it is the third equation that cuts the line down to a point.
            The drawing shows finite portions of infinite planes; the labels identify the
            equations in their original order.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="int-unique-planes" iframe="demos-manim/planes.html?sys=1,1,2,-1:2,1,3,0:0,-2,1,2&amp;radius=3&amp;yaw=38&amp;pitch=24" width="100%" aspect="4:5">
            <shortdescription>Three translucent planes in space sharing exactly one point, (5/3, -4/3, -2/3), with controls to rotate the view and hide planes.</shortdescription>
            <static>
              <image source="images/ch1-unique-planes.svg" width="90%">
                <shortdescription>Three planes have one common point at (5/3,-4/3,-2/3).</shortdescription>
              </image>
            </static>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-no-solution-rr">
      <title>An Inconsistent System</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve the system
          <md>
            <mrow>
              -3x_1 - 9x_2 + x_3 \amp = -9
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              2x_1 + 6x_2 - x_3 \amp = 6
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              x_1 + 3x_2 - x_3 \amp = 2
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                -3 \amp -9 \amp 1 \amp -9 \\
                2 \amp 6 \amp -1 \amp 6 \\
                1 \amp 3 \amp -1 \amp 2
              \end{array} \right]
              \amp \xrightarrow{r_3 \leftrightarrow r_1}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
                2 \amp 6 \amp -1 \amp 6 \\
                -3 \amp -9 \amp 1 \amp -9
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow[3r_1 + r_3]{-2r_1 + r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp -2 \amp -3
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{2r_2 + r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1
              \end{array} \right].
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          The last row says
          <md>
            0x_1 + 0x_2 + 0x_3 = 1, \qquad \text{or} \qquad 0 = 1,
          </md>
          which is impossible, and hence the system is inconsistent (no solution).
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-no-solution-planes">
          <caption>
            Each pair of planes intersects in a line, but the three planes have no
            common point. A solution must satisfy all three equations at once. Switch
            any one plane off and a dashed line of common solutions of the other two
            appears; switch it back on and the line pierces the third plane nowhere.
            (Rotate to look along the three parallel dashed lines.) The labels identify
            the equations in their original order.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="int-no-solution-planes" iframe="demos-manim/planes.html?sys=-3,-9,1,-9:2,6,-1,6:1,3,-1,2&amp;center=0,1,0&amp;radius=4&amp;yaw=55&amp;pitch=25" width="100%" aspect="4:5">
            <shortdescription>Three translucent planes in space that intersect pairwise in three parallel dashed lines but have no common point, with controls to rotate the view and hide planes.</shortdescription>
            <static>
              <image source="images/ch1-no-solution-planes.svg" width="90%">
                <shortdescription>Three planes intersect pairwise in parallel lines and have no common point. A two-dimensional slice at x1=0 shows three separate pairwise crossings.</shortdescription>
              </image>
            </static>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <remark xml:id="rem-inconsistency-test">
      <p>
        In general, a system is inconsistent exactly when the row-echelon form of its
        augmented matrix has a row of the form
        <m>\left[ \begin{array}{cccc|c} 0 \amp 0 \amp \cdots \amp 0 \amp d \end{array} \right]</m>
        with <m>d \neq 0</m>.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <p>
      Row reduction detects every inconsistent system in this one way. Geometrically,
      however, three planes can fail to have a common point in visibly different ways.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="eg-inconsistent-configurations">
      <title>Three Ways to Have No Common Point</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Each of the following systems is inconsistent. For each one, row reduce far
          enough to exhibit the contradictory row, and then describe how the three planes
          are placed relative to one another.
          <ol marker="(a)" cols="3">
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\begin{array}{rcr} x_1 + x_2 + x_3 \amp = \amp 1 \\ 2x_1 + 2x_2 + 2x_3 \amp = \amp 4 \\ -x_1 - x_2 - x_3 \amp = \amp 3 \end{array}</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\begin{array}{rcr} x_1 + x_2 + x_3 \amp = \amp 1 \\ x_1 + x_2 + x_3 \amp = \amp 3 \\ x_1 - x_2 \phantom{{}+ x_3} \amp = \amp 0 \end{array}</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\begin{array}{rcr} x_1 + x_2 \phantom{{}+ x_3} \amp = \amp 1 \\ x_2 + x_3 \amp = \amp 1 \\ x_1 + 2x_2 + x_3 \amp = \amp 3 \end{array}</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          (a) Two replacements clear the first column, and one more tidies up:
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
              2 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 4 \\
              -1 \amp -1 \amp -1 \amp 3
            \end{array} \right]
            \xrightarrow[r_1 + r_3]{-2r_1 + r_2}
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 4
            \end{array} \right]
            \xrightarrow{-2r_2 + r_3}
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
            \end{array} \right].
          </md>
          The second row says <m>0 = 2</m>. Dividing the second equation by <m>2</m> and
          the third by <m>-1</m> shows what happened: the three equations ask the same
          expression <m>x_1 + x_2 + x_3</m> to equal <m>1</m>, <m>2</m>, and <m>-3</m>.
          These are <term>three parallel planes</term>.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-parallel-planes">
          <caption>
            The three planes of part (a) are parallel. There are no dashed lines, because no two
            of the planes meet: switch any one plane off, and the other two still have no
            common point. Rotate the picture until the planes are seen edge-on, as three
            parallel lines.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="int-parallel-planes" iframe="demos-manim/planes.html?sys=1,1,1,1:2,2,2,4:-1,-1,-1,3&amp;radius=3&amp;yaw=125&amp;pitch=12" width="100%" aspect="4:5">
            <shortdescription>Three translucent parallel planes in space, with controls to rotate the view and hide planes.</shortdescription>
            <static>
              <image source="images/ch1-parallel-planes" width="90%">
                <shortdescription>Three parallel planes; no two of them meet.</shortdescription>
              </image>
            </static>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
        <p>
          (b) Here
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
              1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\
              1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \amp 0
            \end{array} \right]
            \xrightarrow[-r_1 + r_3]{-r_1 + r_2}
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\
              0 \amp -2 \amp -1 \amp -1
            \end{array} \right]
            \xrightarrow{r_2 \leftrightarrow r_3}
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp -2 \amp -1 \amp -1 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 2
            \end{array} \right],
          </md>
          and the last row says <m>0 = 2</m>. The first two planes are parallel, as in (a).
          The coefficients <m>1, -1, 0</m> of the third equation are not a multiple of
          <m>1, 1, 1</m>, so the third plane is parallel to neither of them: it cuts each of
          them in a line. This is <term>two parallel planes crossed by a third</term>.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-two-parallel-planes">
          <caption>
            The parallel planes <m>P_1</m> and <m>P_2</m> of part (b) never meet, and <m>P_3</m>
            crosses each of them in a dashed line. Switch <m>P_1</m> or <m>P_2</m> off and the
            two remaining equations have a whole line of common solutions; switch
            <m>P_3</m> off and the system is still inconsistent.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="int-two-parallel-planes" iframe="demos-manim/planes.html?sys=1,1,1,1:1,1,1,3:1,-1,0,0&amp;radius=3&amp;yaw=-150&amp;pitch=40" width="100%" aspect="4:5">
            <shortdescription>Two translucent parallel planes crossed by a third plane along two parallel dashed lines, with controls to rotate the view and hide planes.</shortdescription>
            <static>
              <image source="images/ch1-two-parallel-planes" width="90%">
                <shortdescription>Two parallel planes crossed by a third plane in two parallel lines; no point lies on all three.</shortdescription>
              </image>
            </static>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
        <p>
          (c) Finally,
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
              1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 3
            \end{array} \right]
            \xrightarrow{-r_1 + r_3}
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 2
            \end{array} \right]
            \xrightarrow{-r_2 + r_3}
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1
            \end{array} \right],
          </md>
          and the last row says <m>0 = 1</m>. This time no two of the coefficient lists
          <m>1, 1, 0</m> and <m>0, 1, 1</m> and <m>1, 2, 1</m> are multiples of one another,
          so no two of the planes are parallel: <term>every pair of planes meets in a
          line, but the three lines never meet</term>. This is also the configuration
          drawn in <xref ref="fig-no-solution-planes" text="type-global" />.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-prism-planes">
          <caption>
            No two of the planes of part (c) are parallel. Each pair meets in a dashed line, but
            the three lines are parallel and different. Switch any one plane off and the other
            two share a line. Rotate to look along the dashed lines: the planes then appear
            as the three sides of a triangle.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="int-prism-planes" iframe="demos-manim/planes.html?sys=1,1,0,1:0,1,1,1:1,2,1,3&amp;radius=3&amp;yaw=155&amp;pitch=30" width="100%" aspect="4:5">
            <shortdescription>Three translucent planes in space that meet pairwise in three parallel dashed lines but have no common point, with controls to rotate the view and hide planes.</shortdescription>
            <static>
              <image source="images/ch1-prism-planes" width="90%">
                <shortdescription>Three planes meeting pairwise in three parallel lines, with no common point.</shortdescription>
              </image>
            </static>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
        <p>
          The three echelon forms look alike. To tell the configurations apart, compare the
          coefficient lists of the original equations: all three proportional in (a),
          exactly two proportional in (b), and no two proportional in (c).
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
      These three pictures have one feature in common, and it lets us draw all of them in
      the plane. When a system of three equations in three unknowns is inconsistent, the
      contradictory row uses up one of the three rows, so the coefficient columns contain at
      most two pivots. Replace every constant term by <m>0</m>: the new system has the same
      coefficient columns, hence a free variable, hence a solution <m>(d_1, d_2, d_3)</m> other
      than <m>(0, 0, 0)</m>. Moving a point by any multiple of <m>(d_1, d_2, d_3)</m> changes
      none of the three left-hand sides. So each plane contains, along with any of its
      points, the entire line through that point in this one direction; in part (c), for
      instance, the direction is <m>(1, -1, 1)</m>. Looking along that direction we see every
      plane edge-on, as a line, and every line in which two planes meet end-on, as a point.
      <xref ref="fig-inconsistent-edge-on" text="type-global" /> shows the three views.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-inconsistent-edge-on">
      <caption>
        The three configurations of
        <xref ref="eg-inconsistent-configurations" text="type-global" />, each seen
        edge-on along a direction in which all three planes run. The planes
        <m>P_1</m>, <m>P_2</m>, <m>P_3</m> appear as lines; a dot marks a line in which
        two planes meet, seen end-on. In no picture do all three lines pass through one
        point. Rotating the interactive figures of the example brings each of them into
        its view here.
      </caption>
      <sidebyside widths="30% 30% 30%" margins="auto">
        <figure xml:id="fig-edge-on-parallel">
          <caption>Three parallel planes.</caption>
          <image>
            <shortdescription>Three parallel lines labeled P1, P2, P3 that never cross.</shortdescription>
            <prefigure label="pf-edge-on-parallel" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
              <diagram dimensions="(230,230)" margins="6">
                <coordinates bbox="(-3,-3,3,3)">
                  <group at="edge-on-parallel">
                    <line at="par-P3" endpoints="((-3,0.6),(0.6,-3))" stroke="#606060" thickness="2.5"/>
                    <line at="par-P1" endpoints="((-2.2,3),(3,-2.2))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2.5"/>
                    <line at="par-P2" endpoints="((-1.4,3),(3,-1.4))" stroke="#876801" thickness="2.5"/>
                    <label anchor="(-2.1,-0.3)" alignment="southwest" color="#606060"><m>P_3</m></label>
                    <label anchor="(1.9,-1.1)" alignment="southwest" color="#4A5D23"><m>P_1</m></label>
                    <label anchor="(1.6,0.0)" alignment="northeast" color="#876801"><m>P_2</m></label>
                  </group>
                </coordinates>
                <annotations>
                  <annotation ref="edge-on-parallel" text="Edge-on view of three parallel planes, seen as three parallel lines">
                    <annotation ref="par-P3" text="Plane P 3, a gray line at the lower left"/>
                    <annotation ref="par-P1" text="Plane P 1, an olive line parallel to P 3, in the middle"/>
                    <annotation ref="par-P2" text="Plane P 2, a mustard line parallel to the other two, at the upper right. No two of the lines meet."/>
                  </annotation>
                </annotations>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-edge-on-two-parallel">
          <caption>Two parallel planes crossed by a third.</caption>
          <image>
            <shortdescription>Two parallel lines P1 and P2 crossed by a third line P3 at two separate marked points.</shortdescription>
            <prefigure label="pf-edge-on-two-parallel" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
              <diagram dimensions="(230,230)" margins="6">
                <coordinates bbox="(-3,-3,3,3)">
                  <group at="edge-on-two-parallel">
                    <line at="tp-P1" endpoints="((-3,-1.65),(3,-0.15))" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2.5"/>
                    <line at="tp-P2" endpoints="((-3,0.15),(3,1.65))" stroke="#876801" thickness="2.5"/>
                    <line at="tp-P3" endpoints="((-0.5,-3),(1,3))" stroke="#606060" thickness="2.5"/>
                    <point at="tp-P1P3" p="(0.0267,-0.8933)" fill="#5E011C" size="4.5"/>
                    <point at="tp-P2P3" p="(0.5067,1.0267)" fill="#5E011C" size="4.5"/>
                    <label anchor="(-2.2,-1.45)" alignment="north" color="#4A5D23"><m>P_1</m></label>
                    <label anchor="(-2.2,0.35)" alignment="north" color="#876801"><m>P_2</m></label>
                    <label anchor="(0.85,2.4)" alignment="east" color="#606060"><m>P_3</m></label>
                  </group>
                </coordinates>
                <annotations>
                  <annotation ref="edge-on-two-parallel" text="Edge-on view of two parallel planes crossed by a third plane">
                    <annotation ref="tp-P1" text="Plane P 1, an olive line across the lower half"/>
                    <annotation ref="tp-P2" text="Plane P 2, a mustard line parallel to P 1 across the upper half"/>
                    <annotation ref="tp-P3" text="Plane P 3, a steep gray line crossing both P 1 and P 2"/>
                    <annotation ref="tp-P1P3" text="The line where P 1 and P 3 meet, seen end-on as a point. It is not on P 2."/>
                    <annotation ref="tp-P2P3" text="The line where P 2 and P 3 meet, seen end-on as a point. It is not on P 1."/>
                  </annotation>
                </annotations>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-edge-on-triangle">
          <caption>Each pair of planes meets, but in three different parallel lines.</caption>
          <image>
            <shortdescription>Three lines P1, P2, P3 forming a triangle, with the three corners marked; no point lies on all three lines.</shortdescription>
            <prefigure label="pf-edge-on-triangle" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
              <diagram dimensions="(230,230)" margins="6">
                <coordinates bbox="(-3,-3,3,3)">
                  <group at="edge-on-triangle">
                    <line at="tr-P1" endpoints="((-1.5,-1.2),(1.7,-0.8))" infinite="yes" stroke="#4A5D23" thickness="2.5"/>
                    <line at="tr-P2" endpoints="((-1.5,-1.2),(0.3,1.7))" infinite="yes" stroke="#876801" thickness="2.5"/>
                    <line at="tr-P3" endpoints="((1.7,-0.8),(0.3,1.7))" infinite="yes" stroke="#606060" thickness="2.5"/>
                    <point at="tr-P1P2" p="(-1.5,-1.2)" fill="#5E011C" size="4.5"/>
                    <point at="tr-P1P3" p="(1.7,-0.8)" fill="#5E011C" size="4.5"/>
                    <point at="tr-P2P3" p="(0.3,1.7)" fill="#5E011C" size="4.5"/>
                    <label anchor="(0.1,-1.0)" alignment="south" color="#4A5D23"><m>P_1</m></label>
                    <label anchor="(-0.75,0.0)" alignment="northwest" color="#876801"><m>P_2</m></label>
                    <label anchor="(1.15,0.2)" alignment="northeast" color="#606060"><m>P_3</m></label>
                  </group>
                </coordinates>
                <annotations>
                  <annotation ref="edge-on-triangle" text="Edge-on view of three planes, no two parallel, seen as three lines forming a triangle">
                    <annotation ref="tr-P1" text="Plane P 1, an olive line forming the bottom side of the triangle"/>
                    <annotation ref="tr-P2" text="Plane P 2, a mustard line forming the left side of the triangle"/>
                    <annotation ref="tr-P3" text="Plane P 3, a gray line forming the right side of the triangle"/>
                    <annotation ref="tr-P1P2" text="The line where P 1 and P 2 meet, seen end-on as the lower left corner. It is not on P 3."/>
                    <annotation ref="tr-P1P3" text="The line where P 1 and P 3 meet, seen end-on as the lower right corner. It is not on P 2."/>
                    <annotation ref="tr-P2P3" text="The line where P 2 and P 3 meet, seen end-on as the top corner. It is not on P 1."/>
                  </annotation>
                </annotations>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </sidebyside>
    </figure>

    <example xml:id="eg-infinitely-many-rr">
      <title>A System with Infinitely Many Solutions</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve the system
          <md>
            <mrow>
              2x_1 + x_2 + 3x_3 \amp = 1
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              2x_2 - x_3 + x_1 \amp = 0
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              10x_3 - 6x_2 \amp = 2
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          After ordering the variables as <m>x_1, x_2, x_3</m> in each equation,
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \amp
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 1 \\
                1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp -6 \amp 10 \amp 2
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{r_1 \leftrightarrow r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
                2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp -6 \amp 10 \amp 2
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-2r_1 + r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp -3 \amp 5 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp -6 \amp 10 \amp 2
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-2r_2 + r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp -3 \amp 5 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-\frac{1}{3} r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp -\frac{5}{3} \amp -\frac{1}{3} \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-2r_2 + r_1}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 0 \amp \frac{7}{3} \amp \frac{2}{3} \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp -\frac{5}{3} \amp -\frac{1}{3} \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
              \end{array} \right].
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Columns <m>1</m> and <m>2</m> are pivot columns, so <m>x_1</m> and <m>x_2</m>
          are basic variables, whereas <m>x_3</m> is a free variable. Setting
          <m>x_3 = s</m>, the two nonzero rows read
          <md>
            <mrow>
              x_1 + \tfrac{7}{3}s \amp = \tfrac{2}{3}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              x_2 - \tfrac{5}{3}s \amp = -\tfrac{1}{3}
            </mrow>
          </md>
          and therefore the solution set in parametric form is
          <md>
            <mrow>
              x_1 \amp = \tfrac{2}{3} - \tfrac{7}{3}s
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              x_2 \amp = -\tfrac{1}{3} + \tfrac{5}{3}s
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              x_3 \amp = s, \qquad \text{where } s \in \R.
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-infinitely-many-planes">
          <caption>
            All three planes contain the line <m>x_1=2/3-7s/3</m>, <m>x_2=-1/3+5s/3</m>,
            <m>x_3=s</m>. Every point on this line solves all three equations: move the
            parameter slider to slide a point along the line and watch all three
            equation checks stay true. Switching any one plane off changes nothing about
            the solution set <mdash /> the third equation was redundant. The drawing shows
            finite portions of infinite planes; the labels identify the equations in
            their original order.
          </caption>
          <interactive xml:id="int-infinitely-many-planes" iframe="demos-manim/planes.html?sys=2,1,3,1:1,2,-1,0:0,-6,10,2&amp;radius=3&amp;yaw=38&amp;pitch=24" width="100%" aspect="4:5">
            <shortdescription>Three translucent planes in space all containing one wine-colored line of solutions, with a slider that moves a point along the line and controls to rotate the view and hide planes.</shortdescription>
            <static>
              <image source="images/ch1-infinitely-many-planes.svg" width="90%">
                <shortdescription>Three planes share the entire line x1=2/3-7s/3, x2=-1/3+5s/3, x3=s.</shortdescription>
              </image>
            </static>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
      The pattern is now clear, and it is worth recording as a theorem, since it is the
      main result of the chapter.
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="rem-free-variables-count">
      <title>Existence and Uniqueness of Solutions</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let a system of linear equations have augmented matrix <m>A</m>, and let
          <m>R</m> be a row-echelon form of <m>A</m>.
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Existence.</term> The system is consistent if and only if <m>R</m>
                has no row of the form
                <m>\left[ \begin{array}{cccc|c} 0 \amp 0 \amp \cdots \amp 0 \amp d \end{array} \right]</m>
                with <m>d \neq 0</m>; equivalently, if and only if the augmented column
                is <em>not</em> a pivot column.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <term>Uniqueness.</term> If the system is consistent, then it has a unique
                solution when there are no free variables, and infinitely many solutions
                when there is at least one free variable <mdash /> one parameter for each
                free variable.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          A row <m>[\,0 \; \cdots \; 0 \mid d\,]</m> with <m>d \neq 0</m> stands for the
          equation <m>0 = d</m>, which no values of the variables can satisfy, so a system
          whose echelon form contains such a row is inconsistent. Conversely, suppose there
          is no such row. Then every nonzero row of <m>R</m> has its leading entry in a
          variable column, and the backward phase of the algorithm carries <m>R</m> to a
          reduced row-echelon matrix in which each basic variable appears, with
          coefficient <m>1</m>, in exactly one equation, and in no other. Assign arbitrary
          values to the free variables; each equation then determines the value of its
          basic variable. This produces a solution, so the system is consistent. That
          proves existence.
        </p>
        <p>
          For uniqueness, the same description shows that the solutions are in one-to-one
          correspondence with the choices of values for the free variables. If there are
          no free variables there is exactly one choice, hence one solution. If there is at
          least one free variable, it can take infinitely many values, each giving a
          different solution.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>

    <p>
      The theorem explains, from the point of view of the algorithm, the trichotomy of
      <xref ref="thm-zero-one-infinitely-many" text="type-global"/>: a system has no
      solution, one solution, or infinitely many, and nothing in between. It also has a
      useful consequence that can be read off before any computation: <em>a consistent
      system with more unknowns than equations has infinitely many solutions</em>. For
      there is at most one pivot per row, hence at most <m>m</m> basic variables in a
      system of <m>m</m> equations, and when <m>n > m</m> at least <m>n - m</m> of the
      <m>n</m> variables must be free. We will meet this consequence again in
      <xref ref="sec-homogeneous-systems" text="type-global"/>.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-systems-with-parameters">
    <title>Systems Depending on Parameters</title>

    <p>
      A parameter can play two different roles. In a solution formula such as
      <m>x_3=s</m>, it describes the different solutions of one fixed system. Here,
      <m>a,b,c</m> instead specify the equations themselves: choosing their values
      selects a particular system. Treat them as fixed constants while solving,
      and check when an expression is zero before dividing by it.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="eg-parameters-abc">
      <title>Conditions on the Coefficients</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find all values of <m>a</m>, <m>b</m> and <m>c</m> (or conditions on <m>a</m>,
          <m>b</m> and <m>c</m>) so that the system
          <md>
            <mrow>
              2x_1 + 3x_2 + ax_3 \amp = b
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \phantom{2x_1 + {}} -x_2 + 2x_3 \amp = c
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              x_1 + 3x_2 - 2x_3 \amp = 1
            </mrow>
          </md>
          has (i) a unique solution, (ii) no solutions, and (iii) infinitely many
          solutions. In (i) and (iii), find the solution(s).
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Row reduce the augmented matrix, treating <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, <m>c</m> as
          constants:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                2 \amp 3 \amp a \amp b \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp c \\
                1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1
              \end{array} \right]
              \amp \xrightarrow{r_1 \leftrightarrow r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp c \\
                2 \amp 3 \amp a \amp b
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-2r_1 + r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp c \\
                0 \amp -3 \amp a+4 \amp b-2
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-3r_2 + r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp c \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp a-2 \amp b-2-3c
              \end{array} \right].
            </mrow>
          </md>
          Everything now hinges on the entry <m>a - 2</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>Case (I): <m>a - 2 \neq 0</m>, i.e., <m>a \neq 2</m>.</term>
          We may divide the third row by <m>a-2</m> and continue. Writing
          <m>K = \dfrac{b-2-3c}{a-2}</m> for brevity,
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{\left( \frac{1}{a-2} \right) r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp c \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp K
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp -2 \amp -c \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp K
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{2r_3+r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -c + 2K \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp K
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{2r_3 + r_1}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 3 \amp 0 \amp 1 + 2K \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -c + 2K \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp K
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-3r_2 + r_1}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 + 3c - 4K \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -c + 2K \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp K
              \end{array} \right].
            </mrow>
          </md>
          Hence when <m>a \neq 2</m>, there is a unique solution:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              x_1 \amp = 1 + 3c - 4\left( \frac{b-2-3c}{a-2} \right)
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              x_2 \amp = -c + 2 \left( \frac{b-2-3c}{a-2} \right)
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              x_3 \amp = \frac{b-2-3c}{a-2}.
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>Case (II): <m>a - 2 = 0</m> and <m>b - 2 - 3c \neq 0</m>.</term>
          The row-echelon matrix is
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp c \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp b-2-3c
            \end{array} \right],
          </md>
          whose last row asserts <m>0 = b-2-3c \neq 0</m>. Then the system has no solution.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>Case (III): <m>a - 2 = 0</m> and <m>b - 2 - 3c = 0</m>.</term>
          Now the last row is entirely zero, and <m>x_3</m> is a free variable, so the
          system has infinitely many solutions. Setting <m>x_3 = s</m>, the second row
          gives <m>-x_2 + 2s = c</m>, so <m>x_2 = -c + 2s</m>, and the first row gives
          <md>
            x_1 = 1 - 3x_2 + 2x_3 = 1 - 3(-c + 2s) + 2s = 1 + 3c - 4s.
          </md>
          The solution set in parametric form is
          <md>
            <mrow>
              x_1 \amp = 1 + 3c - 4s
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              x_2 \amp = -c + 2s
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              x_3 \amp = s, \qquad \text{where } s \in \R.
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          In summary: the system has a unique solution when <m>a \neq 2</m>, no solution
          when <m>a = 2</m> and <m>b \neq 2 + 3c</m>, and infinitely many solutions when
          <m>a = 2</m> and <m>b = 2 + 3c</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>
</section>
